WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
Here's the thing nobody tells you about compound interest.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:09.100
For the first several years, it looks almost exactly like nothing is happening.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:14.600
You put in a fixed amount every month, and the balance creeps up in a straight line.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:19.800
That's not a flaw in the math. That's what the early part of an exponential curve looks like.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:25.300
The curve is bending the whole time, you just can't see the bend yet with your eyes.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:31.000
Most people quit around year four or five, right before the bend becomes visible.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:36.500
They conclude the strategy doesn't work, when really they just stopped one chapter too early.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:42.000
If you want a mental model, think of it less like a savings account and more like a lever.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.400
A lever does nothing until you've pushed it far enough for the load to move.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:52.900
Then it moves fast, and it keeps moving fast, and that part is what people remember.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:58.200
So the practical advice is boring: automate the contribution, and stop checking the balance so often.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:03.600
Checking it monthly when the real story only shows up over five-year windows just produces anxiety.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.900
The version of you that benefits from compounding is not the version reading the balance today.
